namespace bo3z::codex
{
	struct Chapter { int book; std::string_view title, image; std::vector<std::string_view> paras; };
	inline constexpr std::array<std::string_view, 2> kBooks{ "The Curse of Alduin", "A Survivor's Guide" };
	inline const std::vector<Chapter> kChapters{
		{ 0, "The Greybeard's Account", "sigil", { "Set down at High Hrothgar by a Greybeard who has broken his silence, for whoever lived through Helgen" } },
		{ 0, "I. Three Words", "helgen", { "Helgen burned, and you lived. But Alduin did not flee: as he rose from the ash he shouted three words into the earth - GRAH DIR LEIN, Battle, Death, World - and every barrow in Skyrim answered. Now when the sun goes down the dead claw their way up to finish what he began. Only the Echo Altars the ancient Tongues raised in every hold can still his voice, and they have gone dark. Wake them, city by city, and their dead will sleep again.", "We heard it on the mountain. Not a shout to break stone, but a Word sung slow, that sank into the earth and stayed there: <b>Grah</b>, battle. <b>Dir</b>, death. <b>Lein</b>, the world. By day it sleeps in the ground. When the sun goes down it wakes, and every barrow, every crypt and every churchyard in the holds remembers that it is owed a war." } },
		{ 0, "II. The Dead Who Answer", "graves", { "From dusk until first light the dead climb out of the earth, more each night and harder to kill. At dawn they fall back to dust. The Nords of old had names for them, and the names are true again:", "<b>Haugbui</b>, the mound-dwellers - slow, many, never tired. <b>Pyre-Thralls</b>, the burned dead, still smouldering. <b>Gravegnawers</b>, quick and starved. <b>Barrow Archers</b> on the walls.", "The <b>Tongues</b> - Ash, Rime and Storm - were shouters once; their dead mouths still hold a word or two. <b>Fen-Wailers</b> scream, and the horde comes to the scream: kill them first. <b>Sewn Thralls</b> burst when they fall. <b>Grave-Hounds</b> run in packs, and the <b>Bone Jotunn</b> is the skeleton of a giant, walking.", "Over them all ride the great ones: the <b>Thanes of the Mound</b>, the <b>Mother of Mounds</b>, <b>Haugrisi the Mound-Giant</b>, <b>the Elk-Crowned</b>, and <b>Grahzah</b>, Alduin's Grave-Wing, a dragon of bone. Look at a wounded thing and a pale bar shows how much life it has left; the great ones' bars burn gold and bear their names." } },
		{ 0, "III. The Echo Altars", "", { "This is not the first time Alduin has shouted the dead awake. In the Dragon War he did it once over the barrows of the Atmorans, and the Tongues - the first men to learn the Voice - answered him. They raised an altar in every hold where the dead marched: a star of violet fire caught in a ring of floating stones, bound with runes of blue, standing on a floor of black stone inlaid with gold.", "The fire is Alduin's own Thu'um, caught and turned back on itself. The runes are the Tongues' answer, holding it there. Every mage who fell in that war gave their last spell to the star, and the star kept it: that is why the altars have so many to give, and why the holds call them <b>Echo Altars</b>.", "When Alduin was cast out of time the altars went dark and sank into the earth, and towns grew over them. At Helgen he returned, and the altars woke - but only half. Each one has been silenced by a dead jarl of old, raised to guard it against the living: a <b>Barrow-Jarl</b>." } },
		{ 0, "IV. Waking an Altar", "wards", { "The altars answer to you. Perhaps it is because you heard the three Words at Helgen and lived; perhaps it is something older. Stand before one and it will set you <b>trials</b> - hold its runic circle while the dead come for you, cull the risen in its streets - and when they are done its Barrow-Jarl rises to stop you. Strike him down and the altar rings with the Tongues' answer. Its city becomes a sanctuary: no dead rise within its walls, and the altar gives you the city's own <b>Thu'um</b>." } },
		{ 0, "V. The Voices of the Holds", "", { "<b>Riverwood</b>: <i>Stormblast</i> (LOK BAH QO, 'Sky Wrath Lightning'). Lightning gathers in your weapon for one devastating blow.", "<b>Solitude</b>: <i>Dance of the Dead</i> (RAAN VAAZ ZOL, 'Animal Tear Zombie'). The fallen rise again to fight at your side.", "<b>Windhelm</b>: <i>Iceborn</i> (IIZ AH VIING, 'Ice Hunter Wing'). The cruel winds of the North shatter the dead around you.", "<b>Markarth</b>: <i>Earthquake</i> (FUS KLO UL, 'Force Sand Eternity'). The earth shakes the dead off their feet, or turns them to stone.", "<b>Riften</b>: <i>Phantom Decoy</i> (FIIK LO SAH, 'Mirror Deceive Phantom'). A ghostly double draws the horde away, then bursts.", "<b>Falkreath</b>: <i>Essence Rip</i> (LAAS RO DAH, 'Life Balance Push'). Tear the life out of the dead to heal your wounds.", "<b>Morthal</b>: <i>Curse</i> (FIIK ZII GRON, 'Mirror Spirit Bind'). What the dead do to you, they suffer back.", "<b>Dawnstar</b>: <i>Wail of the Banshee</i> (FAAS ZAH FRUL, 'Fear Finite Temporary'). A screech that slays the weak and drinks their life.", "<b>Winterhold</b>: <i>Shattersphere</i> (FO MEY GUT, 'Frost Fool Far'). A sphere of elemental ice from the frozen peaks.", "<b>Whiterun</b>: <i>Dragon's Updraft</i>. A whirlwind lifts you high - and with it, a dragon that will carry you." } },
		{ 0, "Epilogue", "", { "Every Echo Altar in Skyrim is awake. Alduin's voice lingers only in the wild places, and it will not fall silent until the World-Eater does.", "<i>- written in the silence of High Hrothgar</i>" } },
		{ 1, "Before Dusk", "", { "Notes left at the Sleeping Giant Inn by one who made it this far. Read them before dusk." } },
		{ 1, "Day and Night", "", { "A day lasts about fifteen minutes. By day the dead sleep: rest, explore, spend what you earned. At dusk they rise in <b>waves</b>, clawing up out of the ground at the same places every night. Each wave brings twenty more than the last: twenty, forty, sixty... The first night has five waves; every night adds one more. The night does not end until the last wave is down - then dawn breaks.", "You cannot sleep or wait while the dead walk." } },
		{ 1, "Septims", "", { "Every risen thing you kill leaves septims. A headshot leaves more, and a kill with a blade or a spell more still. Septims are the only coin the Echo Altars take.", "Watch the dead for tokens: a skull (Insta-Kill), a coin (Double Points), a falling star (it destroys every corpse risen tonight) and a chest (a Fire Sale at the altar)." } },
		{ 1, "The Echo Altar", "", { "Every city has one. Look into its star and press Activate. Its menu does not stop the night.", "<b>Call a spell</b> - the altar turns and gives you one of the spells of the fallen. Each call costs more than the last until dawn. You carry two of its spells; a third asks which one it replaces.", "<b>Call a bow</b> - the altar's second wheel (B, or the right trigger). Six elemental bows: fire, frost, storm, wind, sun and blood. You carry one; a new one takes its place.", "<b>Reforge</b> - make the spell or bow in your hands stronger, three times over. Every tier gives a bow a new attack: the fire bow's arrows burst, then leave burning ground, then a full draw calls down a volley of meteors.", "<b>Perks</b> - lasting gifts. You carry five at most.", "<b>Trials</b> - the quest that wakes the altar. It offers five; take up any three, in any order (keys 6 to 0, or the shoulder buttons and D-pad up). When three are done the city's Barrow-Jarl rises at night near the altar. Slay it and the city is safe for good. Skyrim's difficulty sets how hard the trials are." } },
		{ 1, "The Five Trials", "", { "<b>Ward of the Tongues</b> - stand in the runic circle round the altar while the dead come faster. Step out and the count waits for you.", "<b>The Culling</b> - kill a number of the risen in the city.", "<b>The Unbroken</b> - clear whole waves without falling below half health. Fall below and that wave does not count.", "<b>Trial of Steel</b> - only kills with a melee weapon or your fists count.", "<b>The Long Night's Breath</b> - stay by the altar while the night's waves are cleared." } },
		{ 1, "Spells", "", { "Tap the attack to throw a bolt. Hold it, and both hands gather the spell's full power: the longer you hold, the harder it strikes. Let go too soon and the power gutters out. Magicka comes back fast." } },
		{ 1, "Shouts", "", { "A woken altar teaches you its city's Thu'um. Equip it like any shout. Its cooldown shows under your spells, bottom right." } },
		{ 1, "The Dragon", "", { "Wake Whiterun's altar and a dragon will answer you. Jump and press Activate in mid-air: it swoops down and you take hold of its harness.", "Steer with the stick or WASD. Forward dives, back climbs. Attack to make it breathe fire on the dead below - only the dead, never your friends. At the altar you can make deeper bonds with it." } },
		{ 1, "Echo Stones and the Reliquary", "", { "When a city's altar is freed, an <b>Echo Stone</b> rises behind it. Walk up to it and it gives you a page: <i>The Broken Words</i>, one for each city. Each page says plainly where a part of a <b>wonder weapon</b> waits and how to take it, and your compass marks the place.", "Some parts fall from the Barrow-Jarls. Some wait in reliquaries at old barrows, guarded, and only wake at night. Some must be earned by killing the dead near a barrow's door.", "Take them to <b>High Hrothgar</b>: a sealed door in the great hall leads down to the Reliquary, where each wonder is forged on its pedestal once its three parts are found." } },
		{ 1, "The Wonder Weapons", "", { "A forged wonder is taken up from its pedestal and cast from your hands like the altar's spells: tap to strike, hold both hands to unleash it. One at a time - return it to take another.", "<b>Gravecaller</b> - grave-wisps hunt the dead and feed you their life. Unleashed: three bone warriors claw out of the earth to fight for you.", "<b>Frostbinder Gauntlets</b> - ice binds what it strikes; strike a bound foe and it shatters, cutting those round it. Unleashed: everything near you freezes, then bursts.", "<b>Wolf Queen's Crown</b> - spirit-wolves run down the dead in pairs. Unleashed: the Queen howls and her spectral pack runs at your side.", "<b>Horn of the Barrow-Jarls</b> - a war-cry hurls the dead back. Unleashed: the old war-host charges down the line in front of you." } },
		{ 1, "The Three Words", "", { "When all four wonders are forged, Alduin's three words wake one after another: <b>GRAH</b> at the Word Wall of Grah in the Reach, <b>DIR</b> in the courtyard of Labyrinthian, <b>LEIN</b> on the summit of the Throat of the World. Your compass marks the word that is awake.", "Go there at night. Its dead rise round you in waves; when the last wave falls, the word's bearer rises. Kill it and the word is silenced for good, and its power stays with you: <b>GRAH</b> - everything you wield hits 15% harder; <b>DIR</b> - the night's blows hurt you 20% less; <b>LEIN</b> - your magicka returns half again as fast.", "Leave the place, or let dawn come, and the word falls quiet until you return. Silence all three and the curse of Alduin is broken." } },
		{ 1, "The Hall of the Awakened", "", { "With the curse broken, the Reliquary under High Hrothgar becomes the <b>Hall of the Awakened</b>. Its stations:", "<b>Alduin's Fang</b> - a greatsword cut from the World-Eater's jaw, wreathed in fire - the strongest weapon there is: it burns the dead it strikes and the fire leaps to those beside them, and a power attack looses a tornado of fire. <b>Robes of the First Dragonborn</b> - wear them and spells and wonders cost a quarter less magicka, and it returns faster. <b>The Bone-Wyrm's Skull</b> - your glider's dragon wears Alduin's bones (touch it again to change back).", "<b>Brazier of the Endless Night</b> - while it burns, the night never ends: wave after wave, until you put it out. <b>Seat of the Awakened</b> - begin again: every city falls back under the curse and the nights start over, the dead half again as tough each cycle. Your wonders, bows, powers and the words' gifts stay yours." } },
		{ 1, "Falling", "", { "At every dawn, and whenever an altar wakes, the day is saved. If you fall, you wake at that dawn. In a party, stand over a fallen friend and hold Activate to lift them.", "Good luck. Stay out of the barrows after dark." } },
	};
}
